{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793540386,"nonce":"Q/pY43z7AUDYJySS3znmyTAG+RI=","message":"2026-11-01, live on scarab-dev (kindnet v20260528-9350166c, kind v0.32.0, k8s 1.36.1). How kindnet enforces: table inet kindnet-network-policies queues to userspace (nfqueue 101) only packets whose saddr/daddr is in set podips-v4 — the IPs of Pods some NetworkPolicy selects; everything else is accepted. A long-lived Pod in a deny-all-egress namespace was added to the set within seconds and could not reach the API server (enforcement worked on this cluster after ~4 h of e2e + chaos churn). A Pod that probes at once can get through before its IP enters the set: up.sh's first canary hit exactly that and is now fixed to repeat for 30 s (a26d5941), like the plugin runtime's own until_isolated retry. So the 13 h failure in the AFK run was NOT this startup race alone (the product probe retries); next time it reproduces, check whether the plugin Pod's IP is in podips-v4 (docker exec scarab-dev-control-plane nft list set inet kindnet-network-policies podips-v4) — a missing IP points at kindnet's pod informer / NRI event handling, a present IP with traffic getting through points at the nfqueue userspace verdict (queue flags bypass = fail-open when the userspace consumer is not reading).","files":null}]}